7. Multiple variables

This is where forecasting becomes much more useful commercially.

Example:

Sales isn't determined only by historical sales.

You might have:

Sales = f(history, price, promotion, holiday, inventory, weather, marketing...)

Learn:

Exogenous variables

Regression + time series

Feature importance

Interventions

Promotions

Price effects

External signals


8. Validation

This is probably one of the most important sections for you.

Learn why ordinary:

train_test_split()

is often wrong for time series.

Then learn:

Chronological train/test

Rolling-origin validation

Walk-forward validation

Expanding window

Sliding window

Backtesting

Forecast horizon


9. Evaluation

Know exactly when to use:

MAE

RMSE

MAPE

sMAPE

WAPE

MASE

Bias

Prediction intervals


And understand why MAPE can be problematic when actual values are zero or close to zero.

10. Model selection

Don't ask:

> "Which model is best?"



Ask:

> "Which model performs best for this particular series and forecasting horizon under proper backtesting?"



Then learn:

Baseline comparison

Hyperparameter tuning

Cross-validation

Ensemble forecasting

Model selection

Forecast combination



